{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbZN83ybA3ratFDVVosa", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7367, "cache_read_input_tokens": 11903, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7367}, "output_tokens": 4, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "80b73831-1f11-4083-921e-3901fdd2b565", "uuid": "25716db0-1a87-4edc-816f-d4a7e118123b", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T13:00:03.529Z", "request_id": "req_011CfbZN7hQ7s7hNYoCeh8kD"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbZN83ybA3ratFDVVosa", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Banker's rounding** (also called round-half-to-even, or `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`) changes only one case: a value that sits exactly halfway between two candidates. Instead of always rounding that value up, it rounds to whichever neighbor is **even**.\n\n| Value | Round-half-up | Banker's (half-even) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0.5 | 1 | **0** |\n| 1.5 | 2 | 2 |\n| 2.5 | 3 | **2** |\n| 3.5 | 4 | 4 |\n| 2.345 \u2192 2 dp | 2.35 | **2.34** |\n| 2.355 \u2192 2 dp | 2.36 | 2.36 |\n\nValues that aren't exactly halfway (2.346, 2.3449, and so on) round the same way under both methods.\n\n### Why accountants like it\n\n1. **It removes systematic bias.** Round-half-up always pushes .5 cases upward, so across thousands of transactions the rounded total drifts higher than the true total. Half-even sends about half of the ties up and half down, so the errors tend to cancel and the sum stays close to the unrounded sum.\n\n2. **Totals reconcile better.** When you round line items such as tax, interest or currency conversions and then add them up, half-even makes it more likely that the sum of the rounded parts matches the rounded total. That means fewer penny discrepancies to explain during reconciliation.\n\n3. **Neither side gets favored.** Always rounding up consistently benefits whoever receives the money. Half-even is neutral, which matters for interest, tax collection and fund allocations where fairness can be audited.\n\n4. **It's a standard.** It's the default rounding mode in IEEE 754 floating point. It's also the default in Python's `round()` and `decimal` module, in .NET's `Math.Round`, and it's available as `RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN` in Java's `BigDecimal`. Many financial and regulatory specs either require it or explicitly allow it.\n\n### Caveats\n\n- **It only matters for exact ties.** With binary floating point, a value like 2.675 isn't actually stored as an exact tie, so you'll get surprising results. Use a decimal type for money.\n- **Some rules require a different method.** Certain tax authorities and contracts specify round-half-up, so the rule you're bound by takes priority.\n- **It can surprise people.** Users who expect 2.5 to become 3 may report it as a bug, so it's worth documenting.\n\nRelated to your project: the EngramDB memory store has a recorded decision that **tax calculations in ledgerline use `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`**. Any tax code you touch should keep that convention."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7367, "cache_read_input_tokens": 11903, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7367}, "output_tokens": 4, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "80b73831-1f11-4083-921e-3901fdd2b565", "uuid": "66ecbd23-4eda-4a41-8e27-4937c6adf3ee", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T13:00:12.397Z", "request_id": "req_011CfbZN7hQ7s7hNYoCeh8kD"}
